<PageLayout title='Evolutionary Genomics' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='How do evolutionary processes leave different signatures across a genome?'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>Evolution acts on genomes through multiple processes at once. Mutation produces variation; recombination reshuffles genealogical histories; selection changes the fate of variants; drift preserves or erases lineages; population structure and migration alter who shares ancestry with whom.</p><p>Homogmos Lab approaches the genome as a heterogeneous record rather than a single evolutionary object. This means comparing regions, genealogies and timescales instead of assuming that one tree or one summary statistic describes the whole genome.</p><h2>$ methods</h2><ul><li>Population-genomic summary statistics and window-based analysis</li><li>Phylogenetic and Bayesian inference</li><li>Coalescent reasoning and local TMRCA estimation</li><li>Selection analyses, including codon-based and polymorphism/divergence approaches</li><li>Comparative visualization of genomic landscapes</li></ul><div class="quote">A genome can share one species history while preserving many local genealogical histories.</div></div></section>
</PageLayout>